In this lesson we'll look at how to pull real-world meaning out of the pieces of an exponential model. Think of a curve that shoots up or settles down, and you want to know what each knob actually does. First, the initial value. That's just where the curve starts. You find it by plugging zero into the model, and if the inside shift is zero, the starting height is simply the top multiplier plus the bottom shift. Next, the growth rate. The key is the growth factor per unit step, which is the base raised to the inner power. The minus-one trick turns that factor into a percentage, so one-point-zero-five becomes about five percent each step. From there you can find useful landmarks like doubling time, using the natural log of two over the natural log of the growth factor. For decay, the same idea applies but the factor sits between zero and one, you get a half-life instead, and the long-run behavior flattens out toward the horizontal shift. For growth, it races off to infinity. Now let's see it in action.